<h2>The family, the plot and the room</h2>
<p>The property — a DDA plot in East of Kailash — was allotted to the plaintiff and his mother jointly, making them co-owners. The plaintiff\'s brother married Farida, who came to live in the property; the marriage broke down and the brother claimed to have divorced her in 2008, which she disputed. When the plaintiff sued her for possession of the single first-floor room she occupied, his title case rested on two documents: his mother\'s Will of 2008 bequeathing him the built-up property, and his brother\'s Declaration of Surrender of any share. Both courts below rejected the case, and the second appeal met the same fate.</p>

<h2>Three independent failures</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Proof of the Will</strong><p>The propounder of a Will must prove due execution and attestation. Here, the attesting witness was unreliable and a material witness went unexamined — the suspicious circumstances were never dispelled.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>The one-third rule</strong><p>Under Muslim personal law, a bequest beyond one-third of the estate requires the heirs\' consent. No consent was shown, so even a proved Will could not have carried the whole property to the plaintiff.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Stamp and registration</strong><p>The brother\'s surrender of his interest was unstamped and unregistered, and therefore incapable of transferring rights in immovable property — leaving the plaintiff without the consolidated title he pleaded.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Section 39: the quiet guardian of residence</h2>
<p>The most consequential part of the judgment is its use of Section 39 of the Transfer of Property Act. The provision, a survival of the older Hindu-law doctrine protecting maintenance-holders, provides that where a person has a right to receive maintenance from the profits of immovable property, a transfer of that property does not defeat the right if the transferee has notice of it or the transfer is gratuitous. Farida had asserted her rights in ongoing matrimonial litigation — a maintenance petition under Section 125 CrPC, an FIR under Sections 498A/406 IPC and a complaint under Section 12 of the DV Act — when the surrender was executed. A transfer arranged within the family during that litigation could not be used to strip her of the roof over her head. The reasoning complements the shared-household jurisprudence under Section 17 of the DV Act: even outside that statute, the general law of property protects a dependent\'s residence against strategic intra-family transfers.</p>

<h2>Second-appeal discipline</h2>
<p>A Regular Second Appeal under Section 100 CPC lies only on a substantial question of law. The question framed concerned the refusal of even a limited injunction restraining Farida from occupying other portions of the property; the Court answered it against the appellant because no evidence showed any threatened entry — an injunction guards against proved apprehension, not speculation. With concurrent findings of fact on the Will and the surrender, and no perversity shown, the appeal could not succeed. The Court noted where the dispute really belongs: between co-owners, the remedy is partition or a negotiated settlement, not a possession suit against an occupant protected by law.</p>

<h2>Drafting and strategy lessons</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Mistake in this litigation</th><th>The correct course</th></tr>
<tr><td>Resting title on an unproved Will</td><td>Secure and preserve attesting witnesses; anticipate the suspicious-circumstances inquiry</td></tr>
<tr><td>Using an unstamped, unregistered surrender</td><td>Stamp and register every instrument transferring interests in immovable property</td></tr>
<tr><td>Transferring shares mid-litigation</td><td>Expect Section 39 and lis pendens scrutiny; such transfers rarely defeat dependants\' rights</td></tr>
<tr><td>Suing for possession amid co-ownership</td><td>Partition proceedings determine shares first; possession follows title, not the reverse</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="note">
<p>The appeal was dismissed with pending applications disposed of. The judgment does not decide the disputed divorce or the parties\' eventual shares — those remain open to partition or settlement between the co-owners.</p>
</div>
